WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
The newer benchmarks: SWE-bench Pro and Terminal-Bench 2.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:04.824
0.

3
00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:13.467
SWE-bench Pro (Deng and colleagues at Scale AI, 2025) responds to contamination and saturation.

4
00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:28.808
It has 1,865 problems from 41 actively maintained repositories, and the tasks are deliberately long-horizon: the authors describe work that could take a professional hours to days, often spanning several files.

5
00:00:29.088 --> 00:00:35.723
Crucially, it is split into a public set, a held-out set, and a commercial set drawn from private codebases.

6
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:44.280
Held-out and private tasks can’t have been in anyone’s training data, so a gap between public and held-out scores is itself informative.

7
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.755
Terminal-Bench 2.

8
00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:55.037
0 (Merrill and colleagues, 2026; ICLR 2026) tests a broader skill: getting real work done in a terminal.

9
00:00:55.317 --> 00:01:02.059
Its 89 tasks each come with their own environment, a human-written reference solution, and automated tests.

10
00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:07.181
At publication, frontier agents solved fewer than 65% of tasks.

11
00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:16.592
Because the agent’s harness matters so much in a terminal, results are reported for model and agent combinations, which is the honest way to report them.

12
00:01:16.872 --> 00:01:19.155
Two habits make these leaderboards useful.

13
00:01:19.435 --> 00:01:27.584
First, read the scaffold: the same model scores differently in different harnesses, and vendors often report their best configuration.

14
00:01:27.864 --> 00:01:35.672
Second, read the uncertainty: on 89 tasks, a few points of difference is within noise, as the exercise below shows.

15
00:01:35.952 --> 00:01:54.363
Studio 4 introduced the Agentic Benchmark Checklist (Zhu and colleagues, 2025), whose authors found that task-setup and grading flaws can misestimate agent performance by up to 100% in relative terms; it’s a good list to hold any agent benchmark against.
